WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.913
Reinforcement learning has mastered complex skills,

00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:10.625
but almost always by assuming the environment
resets to a fixed initial state after every episode.

00:00:10.705 --> 00:00:15.419
In the real world, especially in robotics, those
resets are expensive,

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:18.775
requiring human supervision or scripted routines.

00:00:18.855 --> 00:00:23.364
This work, presented at ICML 2023 by Jigang Kim,
Daesol Cho, and H.

00:00:23.444 --> 00:00:26.035
Jin Kim from Seoul National University,

00:00:26.115 --> 00:00:31.241
tackles autonomous reinforcement learning without
any resets and, crucially,

00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:33.090
without any demonstrations.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.802
Their method, called IBC,

00:00:34.882 --> 00:00:41.035
combines an auxiliary agent that fades away as the
main agent improves with a bidirectional

00:00:41.115 --> 00:00:43.980
goal curriculum built on optimal transport.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.774
The result is state-of-the-art performance on
non-episodic benchmarks,

00:00:48.854 --> 00:00:55.760
matching methods that rely on expert data and even
approaching episodic oracle reinforcement learning.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:01.435
Standard reinforcement learning quietly assumes
something that rarely holds outside a

00:01:01.515 --> 00:01:04.217
simulator: that at the end of every episode,

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:07.694
the environment magically resets to its starting state.

00:01:07.774 --> 00:01:12.879
For a physical robot, resetting means human
intervention, scripted reset policies,

00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:15.977
or custom rigs, all of which are slow and costly.

00:01:16.057 --> 00:01:21.415
Autonomous reinforcement learning tries to remove
that assumption by learning from one

00:01:21.495 --> 00:01:23.944
long uninterrupted stream of experience.

00:01:24.024 --> 00:01:29.382
But the existing autonomous methods cheat in a
different way: they rely on prior data,

00:01:29.462 --> 00:01:32.986
like expert demonstrations or example states of interest,

00:01:33.066 --> 00:01:38.361
and they struggle badly in environments where the
interactions that matter are sparse

00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:40.511
and almost never happen by chance.

00:01:40.591 --> 00:01:44.620
What has been missing is an agent that learns truly
from scratch,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.960
with no resets and no demonstrations.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.841
Why is demonstration-free autonomous learning so hard?

00:01:50.921 --> 00:01:52.699
In the non-episodic setting,

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:56.612
an untrained forward agent wanders off to arbitrary
states,

00:01:56.692 --> 00:02:02.249
so every new attempt starts from a wildly
different, often useless initial condition.

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:04.968
That instability makes learning collapse.

00:02:05.048 --> 00:02:09.081
Prior work patched this in ways that reintroduce
human effort.

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:12.529
Some methods still ask for occasional manual resets.

00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:17.504
Others only succeed when the useful interactions
happen to occur by chance.

00:02:17.584 --> 00:02:21.616
And the two most directly comparable methods, VaPRL
and MEDAL,

00:02:21.696 --> 00:02:23.738
both lean on demonstration data,

00:02:23.818 --> 00:02:29.707
either to seed a subgoal curriculum or to define
what the backward agent should return to.

00:02:29.787 --> 00:02:35.875
The motivation here is to build an agent that
provides its own anchor and its own curriculum,

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.860
using nothing but the experience it collects.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.104
The core contribution is a method called IBC,

00:02:43.184 --> 00:02:47.049
short for Implicit and Bidirectional Curriculum.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:50.993
As far as the authors know,

00:02:51.073 --> 00:02:54.937
it is the first algorithm for non-episodic
reinforcement learning that consistently learns

00:02:55.017 --> 00:02:58.881
without any manual resets and without any demonstrations.

00:02:58.961 --> 00:03:02.826
It brings together two ideas.

00:03:02.906 --> 00:03:06.770
The first is a conditionally activated auxiliary
agent that forms an implicit curriculum:

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:10.714
it helps the main agent early on and then gradually
disappears as the main agent becomes

00:03:10.794 --> 00:03:14.659
capable.

00:03:14.739 --> 00:03:18.603
The second is a bidirectional goal curriculum,
grounded in optimal transport,

00:03:18.683 --> 00:03:22.547
that automatically proposes intermediate goals for
both the forward and the backward directions.

00:03:22.627 --> 00:03:26.491
Together these let the agent bootstrap its own
training signal,

00:03:26.571 --> 00:03:30.436
and across six sparse-reward environments IBC beats
methods that rely on expert data while

00:03:30.516 --> 00:03:34.380
approaching the performance of an idealized
episodic oracle.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.144
Here is how IBC works.

00:03:36.224 --> 00:03:40.538
During training the agent constantly alternates
between two roles.

00:03:40.618 --> 00:03:43.666
The forward agent tries to accomplish the task.

00:03:43.746 --> 00:03:50.189
The auxiliary agent brings it back toward a set of
target initial states so it can practice again.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:56.113
The key trick is that the auxiliary agent is
activated only when the forward agent fails,

00:03:56.193 --> 00:03:58.510
so as the forward agent gets better,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.037
the auxiliary agent naturally steps in less and less.

00:04:02.117 --> 00:04:04.167
That is the implicit curriculum.

00:04:04.247 --> 00:04:08.427
On top of this sits the explicit, bidirectional
goal curriculum.

00:04:08.507 --> 00:04:10.957
Rather than relying on demonstrations,

00:04:11.037 --> 00:04:16.814
IBC samples candidate states from its own replay
buffer and frames curriculum generation

00:04:16.894 --> 00:04:18.944
as an optimal transport problem,

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:23.670
specifically a Wasserstein Barycenter augmented
with a value bias term.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:29.395
It solves the resulting bipartite matching with a
Minimum Cost Maximum Flow algorithm,

00:04:29.475 --> 00:04:34.920
producing K intermediate goals for the forward
agent and K for the auxiliary agent.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.842
A Lipschitz-continuity assumption lets the method
relax the true objective into a tractable lower bound,

00:04:41.922 --> 00:04:45.703
and standard Soft Actor-Critic handles the policy updates.

00:04:45.783 --> 00:04:51.028
Remarkably, defining the target distribution needs
only about ten sample states,

00:04:51.108 --> 00:04:56.020
and sometimes just one, rather than thousands of
demonstration transitions.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.464
To test IBC,

00:05:00.544 --> 00:05:04.608
the authors assembled six sparse-reward
environments spanning both manipulation and locomotion.

00:05:04.688 --> 00:05:08.752
Two of them, Tabletop Manipulation and Sawyer Door,
come from EARL,

00:05:08.832 --> 00:05:12.897
an established benchmark for autonomous
reinforcement learning.

00:05:12.977 --> 00:05:17.041
The other four are Fetch Pick and Place, Fetch
Push, Fetch Reach, and Point-U-Maze,

00:05:17.121 --> 00:05:21.185
which are standard MuJoCo-based OpenAI Gym tasks
that the authors modified for the reset-free,

00:05:21.265 --> 00:05:25.329
non-episodic setting.

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:29.473
Evaluation follows the EARL protocol: the agent is
spawned once,

00:05:29.553 --> 00:05:33.617
interacts continually, and is reset only rarely,

00:05:33.697 --> 00:05:37.762
after hundreds of thousands of steps.

00:05:37.842 --> 00:05:41.906
Performance is measured as the deployed policy
evaluation metric,

00:05:41.986 --> 00:05:46.050
reported at ten-thousand-step intervals and
averaged across five random seeds.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:52.611
The headline result is that IBC reaches
state-of-the-art success rates across all six environments,

00:05:52.691 --> 00:05:55.521
and it does so without a single demonstration.

00:05:55.601 --> 00:05:57.481
It outperforms VaPRL and MEDAL,

00:05:57.561 --> 00:06:01.908
two strong baselines that actually use near-expert
demonstration data,

00:06:01.988 --> 00:06:07.474
and in many tasks its success rate is comparable to
the oracle: a standard reinforcement

00:06:07.554 --> 00:06:09.688
learning agent trained in the easy,

00:06:09.768 --> 00:06:11.838
fully-resettable episodic setting.

00:06:11.918 --> 00:06:16.771
The demonstration-based baselines especially falter
in the Fetch environments,

00:06:16.851 --> 00:06:22.463
where the task-relevant interactions are sparse and
the evaluation goals are spread across

00:06:22.543 --> 00:06:25.562
a whole region rather than a few discrete points.

00:06:25.642 --> 00:06:30.052
And when VaPRL is stripped of its demonstrations
for a fair comparison,

00:06:30.132 --> 00:06:32.140
its performance drops noticeably,

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:36.630
underscoring just how much the prior methods depend
on that extra data.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:41.093
The ablation study peels the method apart to show
that both pieces earn their keep.

00:06:41.173 --> 00:06:45.256
First, removing the bidirectional curriculum causes
a consistent drop in performance,

00:06:45.336 --> 00:06:49.419
demonstrating the value of gradually guiding the
agent from easy initial states and goals to harder ones.

00:06:49.499 --> 00:06:53.582
Then, further removing the auxiliary agent,

00:06:53.662 --> 00:06:57.745
which reduces the method to naive reset-free
reinforcement learning,

00:06:57.825 --> 00:07:01.908
causes an additional and larger drop, particularly
in the object-manipulation tasks.

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:06.072
The gains are task-dependent: the bidirectional
curriculum matters little in the simple

00:07:06.152 --> 00:07:10.235
Tabletop Manipulation environment,

00:07:10.315 --> 00:07:14.398
and the auxiliary agent is less helpful in Point-U-Maze,

00:07:14.478 --> 00:07:18.561
where the start is already far from the goal.

00:07:18.641 --> 00:07:22.724
The authors also confirm the implicit curriculum
behaves as intended: the fraction of

00:07:22.804 --> 00:07:26.887
episodes the auxiliary agent intervenes in falls
toward zero once the forward agent is

00:07:26.967 --> 00:07:31.050
fully trained.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.603
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:07:33.683 --> 00:07:36.949
IBC was tested on six sparse-reward environments,

00:07:37.029 --> 00:07:41.729
two from the established EARL benchmark and four
adapted MuJoCo tasks,

00:07:41.809 --> 00:07:43.914
each run over five random seeds.

00:07:43.994 --> 00:07:48.967
To define its target distribution, it needs only
about ten example states,

00:07:49.047 --> 00:07:50.538
and sometimes just one,

00:07:50.618 --> 00:07:57.025
compared with the thousands of demonstration
transitions that prior autonomous methods require.

00:07:57.105 --> 00:08:01.190
And despite using zero demonstrations and zero
manual resets,

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:07.882
it reaches success rates comparable to an oracle
agent trained in the far easier episodic setting.

00:08:07.962 --> 00:08:12.730
In short: none of the usual crutches, and roughly
the same performance.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.308
The takeaway is simple to remember.

00:08:15.388 --> 00:08:21.558
IBC shows that an agent can learn robotic
manipulation and locomotion tasks entirely on its own,

00:08:21.638 --> 00:08:26.961
with no environment resets and no demonstrations,
by generating its own curriculum.

00:08:27.041 --> 00:08:34.186
It does this with two cooperating ideas: an
auxiliary agent that anchors the learner early and then fades away,

00:08:34.266 --> 00:08:41.607
and a bidirectional goal curriculum built on
optimal transport that keeps proposing achievable intermediate goals.

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:45.123
The result matches methods that depend on expert data.

00:08:45.203 --> 00:08:52.413
The main caveats are that it assumes reversible
environments and still needs a human to specify a sparse reward,

00:08:52.493 --> 00:08:55.863
and the authors point to a fully reward-free version,

00:08:55.943 --> 00:09:00.290
hinted at by their C-learning experiments, as the
natural next step.
